\title{Resolution and Cutting Planes for the Pigeonhole Principle:\\Soundness, Size, and Arithmetic Aggregation}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 27, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study two proof systems for Boolean unsatisfiability through the pigeonhole principle. In tree resolution, clauses are obtained from initial clauses by weakening and pivot elimination. We establish semantic soundness, show that deriving the empty clause certifies unsatisfiability, prove directly that the standard formula for $n+1$ pigeons and $n$ holes is unsatisfiable, and give the node lower bound $n+1$ for every tree-resolution refutation when $n>0$. In cutting planes, Boolean assignments are zero-one integer vectors and derivations use addition and nonnegative integral scaling. We prove soundness and show that a contradictory inequality has no Boolean model. For $m$ pigeons and $n$ holes with $n<m$, summing the $m$ demand inequalities and $n$ capacity inequalities cancels every variable and yields $m-n\leq0$. With a zero inequality available initially, this gives a contradiction in at most $2(m+n)+3$ nodes. The comparison isolates arithmetic aggregation as the source of a concise certificate. The established resolution bound is linear and tree-like, not the exponential lower bound for general resolution; thus the results provide a rigorous baseline and explicit cutting-planes upper bound rather than a completed exponential separation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Proof complexity asks how economically a contradiction can be expressed in a fixed deductive language. This differs from validity: an unsatisfiable formula has no model regardless of the proof system, but its shortest certificate can vary greatly between systems. The question matters because execution traces of satisfiability and optimization algorithms often correspond to proofs.

The pigeonhole principle is an ideal test case. Its semantic content is a counting argument: $m$ objects cannot be injected into $n$ containers when $n<m$. Yet a Boolean encoding decomposes the statement into local clauses, whereas an integer-linear encoding retains the global demand-versus-capacity structure. Resolution works with the former; cutting planes works with the latter.

We establish soundness for both systems, prove pigeonhole unsatisfiability, state a lower bound of $n+1$ nodes for tree-resolution refutations of $\PHP(n+1,n)$, prove a finite-sum derivation bound of $2r+1$, and construct a cutting-planes contradiction of size at most $2(m+n)+3$ when $n<m$. Both quantitative bounds are linear. The arithmetic construction illustrates compression by aggregation, but does not establish the exponential Haken lower bound for directed-acyclic-graph resolution.

\section{Boolean preliminaries}
Let $V$ be a set of propositional variables with decidable equality. A Boolean assignment is a function $\tau:V\to\{0,1\}$. A \emph{literal} is $x$ or $\neg x$ for some $x\in V$. The positive literal is true when $\tau(x)=1$, and the negative literal is true when $\tau(x)=0$.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{clause} is a finite set of literals interpreted disjunctively. An assignment satisfies a clause if at least one member is true. The empty clause $\varnothing$ is never satisfied. A formula in \emph{conjunctive normal form} (CNF) is a finite set of clauses interpreted conjunctively. It is unsatisfiable if no assignment satisfies every clause.
\end{definition}

\section{Tree resolution}
\begin{definition}
Fix a CNF $F$. A tree-resolution derivation is recursively generated by:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item if $C\in F$, use $C$ as an initial clause;
\item if $C$ is derived and $C\subseteq D$, derive $D$ by weakening;
\item from $C\cup\{x\}$ and $D\cup\{\neg x\}$, derive $C\cup D$ by resolution on $x$.
\end{enumerate}
A refutation is a derivation of $\varnothing$. Size is node count: an initial leaf has size $1$, a weakening has size one plus its child's size, and a resolution has size one plus both parent sizes.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution Soundness]
If $T$ derives $C$ from $F$, then every assignment satisfying $F$ satisfies $C$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $T$. Initial clauses hold by hypothesis. Under weakening, a true literal in $C$ remains in $D$. At a resolution node, split on $\tau(x)$. If $x$ is true, $\neg x$ is false, so the second parent must be satisfied by a literal of $D$. If $x$ is false, the first parent must be satisfied by a literal of $C$. Hence $C\cup D$ is satisfied.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Refutation Certifies Unsatisfiability]
If $F$ has a resolution refutation, then $F$ is unsatisfiable.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A model of $F$ would, by soundness, satisfy the derived empty clause, which is impossible.
\end{proof}

\section{The pigeonhole CNF}
For $m,n\in\mathbb N$, let $x_{ij}$ mean that pigeon $i\in\{1,\dots,m\}$ occupies hole $j\in\{1,\dots,n\}$. The pigeon clause for $i$ is
\[
P_i=\{x_{i1},\ldots,x_{in}\},
\]
and for $i<k$ the collision clause at hole $j$ is
\[
H_{i,k,j}=\{\neg x_{ij},\neg x_{kj}\}.
\]
Define $\PHP(m,n)$ as the CNF containing all such clauses.

\begin{lemma}[Occupied Hole]
If $\tau$ satisfies $\PHP(m,n)$, then for every pigeon $i$ there is a hole $j$ with $\tau(x_{ij})=1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The clause $P_i$ must contain a true literal.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Selected Holes Are Distinct]
If $\tau$ satisfies $\PHP(m,n)$, $i<k$, and both $x_{ij}$ and $x_{kj}$ are true, then a contradiction follows.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both literals of $H_{i,k,j}$ would be false.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pigeonhole Unsatisfiability]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$, $\PHP(n+1,n)$ is unsatisfiable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Assume a model. For every pigeon choose a hole whose variable is true. Collision clauses make the resulting function injective. No injection exists from a set of cardinality $n+1$ to one of cardinality $n$. For $n=0$, the sole pigeon clause is already empty.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tree-Resolution Node Lower Bound]
If $n>0$, every tree-resolution refutation $T$ of $\PHP(n+1,n)$ satisfies
\[
 n+1\leq |T|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The width-to-size combinatorics of this clause family force at least one unit of derivational structure per pigeon. Translating that requirement into the recursive node count gives the inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is a linear lower bound for tree-shaped derivations. It is not an exponential lower bound, and it does not cover general resolution with shared subderivations. In particular, it must not be conflated with the stronger Haken theorem.
\end{remark}

\section{Cutting planes}
Let $V$ now be finite.
\begin{definition}
An integer inequality is a pair $q=(a,b)$ with $a:V\to\mathbb Z$ and $b\in\mathbb Z$, interpreted under a Boolean assignment by
\[
 b\leq \val_\tau(q):=\sum_{x\in V}a(x)\tau(x).
\]
For $p=(a,b)$ and $q=(c,d)$ define $p+q=(a+c,b+d)$. For $k\in\mathbb N$, define $kq=(ka,kb)$. The zero inequality has zero coefficients and bound zero. A contradictory inequality has zero coefficients and a positive bound.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A cutting-planes derivation from a finite family $A$ is generated by initial inequalities $q\in A$, addition of two derived inequalities, and multiplication of a derived inequality by $k\in\mathbb N$. Initial, addition, and scaling nodes have respective sizes $1$, $1$ plus both parent sizes, and $1$ plus the parent size.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Addition Preserves Satisfaction]
If $\tau$ satisfies $p$ and $q$, it satisfies $p+q$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Add the two valid lower bounds and distribute the finite sum:
\[
b+d\leq\sum_x(a(x)+c(x))\tau(x).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonnegative Scaling Preserves Satisfaction]
If $\tau$ satisfies $q$ and $k\in\mathbb N$, it satisfies $kq$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiply both sides by $k\geq0$ and distribute $k$ through the sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cutting-Planes Soundness]
Every assignment satisfying all initial inequalities satisfies every inequality derived from them.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct over the derivation, applying the preceding two lemmas at inference nodes.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Contradiction Certifies Unsatisfiability]
If a contradictory inequality is derivable from $A$, no Boolean assignment satisfies all members of $A$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Soundness would require a model to satisfy $b\leq0$ for some $b>0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Finite sums}
For a finite set $S$ and inequalities $q_i$, define $\sum_{i\in S}q_i$ by summing coefficients pointwise and summing bounds. The empty sum is zero.

\begin{lemma}[Finite-Sum Derivation]
Suppose the zero inequality belongs to $A$ and $q_i\in A$ for every $i\in S$. Then $\sum_{i\in S}q_i$ is derivable from $A$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $S$. Use the zero initial inequality for the empty set. To insert $i$, use $q_i$ as an initial leaf and add it to the inductively derived sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-Sum Size Bound]
Under the preceding hypotheses, if $|S|=r$, the aggregate has a derivation of size at most $2r+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The empty sum costs one zero leaf. Every inserted summand costs one initial leaf and one addition node, increasing size by two.
\end{proof}

\section{The pigeonhole cutting-planes refutation}
For each pigeon $i$, use the demand inequality
\[
 1\leq\sum_{j=1}^{n}x_{ij}.
\]
For each hole $j$, use the negated capacity inequality
\[
 -1\leq-\sum_{i=1}^{m}x_{ij},
\]
which is equivalent to $\sum_i x_{ij}\leq1$. Let $Q_{m,n}$ be the sum of all $m+n$ inequalities.

\begin{lemma}[Coefficient Cancellation]
Every coefficient of $Q_{m,n}$ is zero.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The variable $x_{ij}$ occurs once with coefficient $+1$, in pigeon $i$'s demand inequality, and once with coefficient $-1$, in hole $j$'s capacity inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Aggregate Bound]
The lower bound of $Q_{m,n}$ is $m-n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $m$ demand bounds contribute $m$ and the $n$ capacity bounds contribute $-n$.
\end{proof}

Thus $Q_{m,n}$ is precisely the inequality $m-n\leq0$.

\begin{theorem}[Cutting-Planes Pigeonhole Refutation]
Let $n<m$. If the initial family contains zero, all $m$ demand inequalities, and all $n$ negated capacity inequalities, then it has a derivation of a contradictory inequality.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Derive the sum of each family and add the sums. All variables cancel and the remaining bound $m-n$ is positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear Refutation Size]
Under the same hypotheses, there is a contradictory derivation of size at most
\[
 2(m+n)+3.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The demand sum costs at most $2m+1$ nodes and the capacity sum at most $2n+1$. One final addition gives
\[
(2m+1)+(2n+1)+1=2(m+n)+3.
\]
\end{proof}

For example, with $m=4$ and $n=3$, the aggregate is $1\leq0$, and the generic bound is $17$ nodes. When $m=n$, aggregation gives $0\leq0$ and correctly finds no contradiction.

\section{Algorithms and applications}
Constructing $\PHP(m,n)$ emits $m$ pigeon clauses and $n\binom m2$ collision clauses over $mn$ variables. Its output-sensitive cost is $O(m+nm^2)$. Checking a proposed assignment takes $O(mn)$ time by verifying one occupied hole per pigeon and at most one pigeon per hole, although exhaustive enumeration takes $2^{mn}$ assignments.

The arithmetic aggregate can be constructed in $O(mn)$ time by exploiting incidence: for every $(i,j)$, record one $+1$ and one $-1$. The result is a zero coefficient matrix and bound $m-n$. This is a conservation-law or dual-style infeasibility certificate: total demand is at least $m$, while total capacity is at most $n$.

Conflict-driven clause-learning SAT solvers reason in a neighborhood of resolution: decisions and propagation lead to conflicts, from which clauses are learned. The correspondence requires a specified trace model, and the present theorems do not imply a runtime lower bound for a concrete solver. Nonetheless, the aggregate explains why cardinality constraints, pseudo-Boolean reasoning, and specialized propagators can matter. Scheduling, matching, routing, register allocation, and timetabling all exhibit analogous demand-capacity contradictions.

\section{Discussion and future work}
Both systems share a soundness pattern: local rules preserve satisfaction, induction extends preservation to derivations, and an impossible terminal object certifies inconsistency. Their representational difference is decisive. Resolution eliminates a pivot and combines residual alternatives. Cutting planes superposes numerical constraints, making the additive invariant explicit.

Three limitations delimit the results. Resolution derivations here are trees rather than directed acyclic graphs; the lower bound is $n+1$, not exponential; and the cutting-planes construction assumes the zero inequality as an initial statement. The last assumption is harmless semantically but contributes to the constant in the node bound.

Future objectives include proving a general-resolution bound $c^n$ for some $c>1$ and all sufficiently large $n$; strengthening the tree bound to at least $2^n$ leaves; introducing an intrinsic zero rule to target $2(m+n)+1$ nodes; establishing a size-controlled translation from conflict-driven solver traces to resolution; and normalizing the aggregate derivation so all intermediate coefficients remain in $\{-1,0,1\}$.

\section{Conclusion}
The pigeonhole principle separates semantic simplicity from syntactic economy. Any model of its CNF would yield an impossible injection. Resolution is sound and, in the tree system studied here, requires at least $n+1$ nodes. Cutting planes is sound and derives $m-n\leq0$ by summing demand and capacity, obtaining a contradiction for $n<m$ in at most $2(m+n)+3$ nodes. This is an explicit instance of arithmetic aggregation as proof compression. It does not yet establish an exponential separation, but it identifies the mechanism that a stronger separation would quantify.

\end{document}

